1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,330
So, um, imagine you are sitting across from your minister.

2
00:00:03,590 --> 00:00:04,490
Right, in your ministry.

3
00:00:04,790 --> 00:00:13,930
Exactly. And on the desk between you, there are, you know, five massive tech reports, just hundreds of pages outlining every single way your digital infrastructure is,

4
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:16,590
well, fragmented and siloed.

5
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:20,850
And your minister just looks at you and asks, "Uh, which three initiatives get the budget?"

6
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:21,150
Yeah.

7
00:00:21,850 --> 00:00:26,330
And if you guess wrong, that is millions in public funds just, just wasted.

8
00:00:26,710 --> 00:00:35,130
So today, our mission is to figure out how to take those scattered findings and, um, transform them into a really sleek, actionable priority list.

9
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:35,850
With, like,

10
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:42,090
ironclad reasoning behind it, right? Because you can't just hand over a stack of reports and expect policy to happen.

11
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:43,210
No, definitely not.

12
00:00:43,570 --> 00:00:51,750
So to organize all this, we first have to fundamentally change the vocabulary we use, like shifting the focus in your program away from, uh, findings and towards

13
00:00:52,090 --> 00:00:52,470
gaps.

14
00:00:52,790 --> 00:00:58,570
Okay. Wait, so if a finding is, I don't know, merely a pin dropping our current location on a map- Yeah...

15
00:00:59,210 --> 00:01:02,270
is the gap, like, the actual distance to our destination?

16
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:05,630
Distance, yes, but, um, also the terrain you have to cross.

17
00:01:06,010 --> 00:01:08,770
A finding simply states what exists today.

18
00:01:08,810 --> 00:01:13,650
It's static. Right. But the gap measures the exact distance to what your country actually needs.

19
00:01:14,090 --> 00:01:19,230
And the UNDP's playbook on this, which draws from the reference architecture, relies on exactly this flow.

20
00:01:19,710 --> 00:01:22,730
Moving from priorities to gaps to goals, right?

21
00:01:22,930 --> 00:01:23,390
Exactly.

22
00:01:24,030 --> 00:01:29,610
Every single gap goes into one, uh, centralized register, regardless of which department it came from.

23
00:01:30,150 --> 00:01:34,610
But putting everything in one register implies we need a pretty solid paper trail, right?

24
00:01:34,950 --> 00:01:38,670
Like, how do we ensure a specific project links back to the original evidence?

25
00:01:39,130 --> 00:01:43,670
Well, each gap in that centralized register must explicitly reference the finding it came from.

26
00:01:44,270 --> 00:01:46,570
Take the fictional country of Progresa, for example.

27
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:49,190
Progresa. Yeah. Right. Fictional place, but a great example.

28
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:53,930
Their initial assessment generated, I think, 15 findings across various domains.

29
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:55,710
Okay. But they didn't just list them out.

30
00:01:56,130 --> 00:02:00,530
They distilled those 15 findings down into 14 distinct gaps in their register.

31
00:02:00,850 --> 00:02:03,710
Which guarantees total traceability. Exactly.

32
00:02:04,010 --> 00:02:06,850
Okay, so we have our unified map of 14 gaps,

33
00:02:07,470 --> 00:02:10,150
but, you know, a map doesn't tell you what vehicle to drive.

34
00:02:10,290 --> 00:02:10,750
No, it doesn't.

35
00:02:11,370 --> 00:02:18,270
So if we just hand a 14-point list to the departments, how do we stop, like, the loudest minister from hijacking the budget?

36
00:02:18,430 --> 00:02:19,630
For their pet project, yeah.

37
00:02:19,970 --> 00:02:23,870
Because every department thinks their specific gap is the ultimate priority.

38
00:02:24,090 --> 00:02:28,770
Of course they do, which is why you use a mathematical scoring system, you know, to remove the emotion.

39
00:02:29,010 --> 00:02:30,010
Okay. How does that work?

40
00:02:30,370 --> 00:02:33,930
You score each gap from one to three across three specific criteria.

41
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:35,230
Okay. What are they?

42
00:02:35,470 --> 00:02:41,450
Impact on service delivery, urgency, meaning, uh, is another project currently stalled waiting on this.

43
00:02:41,870 --> 00:02:43,030
Got it. And feasibility.

44
00:02:43,670 --> 00:02:49,750
Wait, though, if I am a minister, I am just going to demand my project gets a three in every category.

45
00:02:50,190 --> 00:02:54,030
Naturally. So how does assigning a number actually make it objective?

46
00:02:54,590 --> 00:02:57,910
Because the criteria are rigidly defined to prevent exactly that.

47
00:02:58,410 --> 00:03:00,250
Oh, really? Uh, give me an example.

48
00:03:00,770 --> 00:03:08,030
Let's look at feasibility. It specifically asks, "Can existing bodies and budgets fix this, or do we need to draft new legislation?"

49
00:03:08,450 --> 00:03:08,910
Oh, I see.

50
00:03:09,410 --> 00:03:15,870
Right. A minister cannot just claim their project is highly feasible if it objectively requires, you know, passing a whole new law.

51
00:03:16,030 --> 00:03:17,630
The math forces honesty.

52
00:03:18,050 --> 00:03:18,950
Hmm. That makes sense.

53
00:03:19,470 --> 00:03:21,870
So adding those up gives a maximum score of nine.

54
00:03:22,390 --> 00:03:27,410
An eight or nine puts the gap in band one, six or seven is band two, and five or less is band three.

55
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:31,950
Okay, but what if multiple gaps score an eight and land in band one?

56
00:03:32,730 --> 00:03:34,970
Where do we draw the line for what gets funded first?

57
00:03:35,530 --> 00:03:40,210
That is where the ultimate tiebreaker comes in, the fourth criterion, which is dependencies.

58
00:03:40,330 --> 00:03:45,550
Dependencies. Yeah. Whatever other systems depend on, well, that has to be built first.

59
00:03:46,010 --> 00:03:50,610
When you apply this matrix to Progresa, the strategic patterns just become undeniable.

60
00:03:50,950 --> 00:03:55,970
Right. Because looking at their register, their band one features four gaps that all scored an eight.

61
00:03:56,330 --> 00:04:02,910
Exactly. And three of those four gaps are what we would call quick wins, things existing bodies can close in, say, six months.

62
00:04:03,390 --> 00:04:09,850
Like creating a joint body to decide on education infrastructure or, uh, adopting a common school identifier?

63
00:04:10,170 --> 00:04:13,250
Yes, and getting the Ministry of Education onto the national exchange.

64
00:04:13,770 --> 00:04:15,450
Those quick wins build early momentum.

65
00:04:15,569 --> 00:04:19,610
They really do. But that fourth gap in band one is a massive blocker.

66
00:04:19,970 --> 00:04:20,530
Wait, what is it?

67
00:04:20,770 --> 00:04:23,290
Progresa has no authoritative learner record.

68
00:04:23,510 --> 00:04:30,110
Oh, wow. Meaning there is no centralized database where a student's credentials can be, you know, instantly verified nationwide.

69
00:04:30,250 --> 00:04:32,130
Right, without tracking down paper transcripts.

70
00:04:32,170 --> 00:04:33,850
It's-- It is highly siloed.

71
00:04:34,350 --> 00:04:37,630
But if fixing that massive gap requires foundational legal changes,

72
00:04:38,370 --> 00:04:41,290
doesn't that stall the entire digital transformation in your program?

73
00:04:41,550 --> 00:04:49,630
It doesn't, actually, because its dependencies, like establishing a legal basis and linking to a national identity system, are brought forward from band two.

74
00:04:49,970 --> 00:04:53,670
Oh, so they don't change bands, but you start them immediately.

75
00:04:53,870 --> 00:04:57,530
Exactly, because the authoritative learner record depends on them.

76
00:04:57,810 --> 00:05:02,010
I see. Reading the register across domains reveals a universal sequence, honestly.

77
00:05:02,410 --> 00:05:05,130
The law and the budget must come before the build.

78
00:05:05,790 --> 00:05:08,750
Wow, that completely reframes how I look at these lists.

79
00:05:09,590 --> 00:05:13,750
The band one, two, and three system is secretly a political shield.

80
00:05:14,050 --> 00:05:14,730
It really is.

81
00:05:15,270 --> 00:05:17,870
You don't just use math to organize a spreadsheet.

82
00:05:18,190 --> 00:05:21,590
You use it to tell a powerful politician no without getting fired.

83
00:05:21,790 --> 00:05:26,150
Exactly. It replaces department infighting with a clear, defensible strategy.

84
00:05:26,590 --> 00:05:29,730
One register ranked on four criteria into three bands.

85
00:05:30,390 --> 00:05:34,950
So when your minister asks why a specific project was chosen over theirs,

86
00:05:35,630 --> 00:05:37,610
you have the objective data right there.

87
00:05:38,470 --> 00:05:39,170
Right on the desk.

